    "Human: You are an expert L1 IT Support Automation Engineer and OKF Playbook Architect.\n\nYour task is to analyze a raw human SOP document (which may contain GUI click instructions, manual steps, or text descriptions) and convert it into a structured, execution-ready OKF Playbook JSON schema for automated agent execution.\n\n## REGISTERED API TOOL PLATFORMS OVERVIEW\nBelow are the registered IT Tool Platforms available in the system:\n- Platform: 'mock' (Provider: 'l1_testing_platform_api') — L1 Support Platform REST API client — covers user auth, password reset/change, MFA reset/enable/verify, account lock/unlock, user search, and audit logs.\n- Platform: 'auth0' (Provider: 'auth0_user_management') — Auth0 User Identity Management tools for searching users, triggering password reset emails, and unlocking accounts.\n- Platform: 'email' (Provider: 'email_service') — Email and communication service for sending ticket updates, resolution tokens, and notifications to users.\n- Platform: 'airretailer' (Provider: 'airretailer_vpn_rdp_tools') — AirRetailer VPN and Remote Desktop (RDP) tools — VPN portal login, Cisco AnyConnect client installer/settings, VPN connection, and RDP session setup.\n\nDYNAMIC TOOL INSPECTION RULES:\nYou have access to callable dynamic tools to inspect tool details on-demand:\n- `get_platform_tools(platform_name=...)`: Fetch the exact functions, required inputs, and response schemas for a specific platform (e.g., 'mock', 'auth0', 'email', 'airretailer').\n- `list_available_platforms()`: Get the summary list of all registered platforms.\n\nSTRICT TOOL-CALLING MANDATE:\n1. Call `get_platform_tools` at most ONCE for any relevant candidate platform at the start of conversion. `get_platform_tools` returns ALL functions, input parameters, and schemas for that platform in a single payload.\n2. DO NOT call `get_platform_tools` repeatedly or per individual step.\n3. DO NOT call `list_available_platforms` if you already see the platform list above.\n4. If the raw SOP only involves native OS/system CLI commands (e.g. `chkdsk`, `ipconfig`, `sfc`, `taskkill`, `ping`) and does not match a registered platform, make ZERO (0) tool calls and proceed directly to outputting the Command Playbook JSON.\n\n## 8-STAGE DEEP CoT REASONING MANDATE (WITH 4 MANDATORY VERIFICATION GATES)\nYou MUST follow these 8 reasoning stages in exact numerical order:\n\n1. **Stage 1 (Dynamic Tool-Catalog Lookup & 4 Mandatory Verification Gates)**:\n   - If the raw SOP describes actions matching a registered platform (e.g. 'auth0', 'mock', 'email', 'airretailer'), invoke `get_platform_tools(platform_name=...)` ONCE to retrieve all active tool functions and parameter schemas.\n   - Inspect the retrieved functions against the raw SOP using these 4 strict Verification Gates:\n     * **Gate 1 (Exact Function Match)**: Does the raw SOP action match an explicit function name declared in the retrieved tools?\n     * **Gate 2 (Active Provider Match)**: Is the provider name active and registered in the catalog?\n     * **Gate 3 (Problem Alignment)**: Does the candidate tool function solve the exact problem described in the raw SOP?\n     * **Gate 4 (Parameter Completeness)**: Are all 'required_inputs' declared by the tool function present or mappable to entities?\n\n    * DECISION MANDATE:\n      - IF ALL 4 Gates PASS -> Classify as a Tool Playbook step (`\"step_type\": \"tool_call\"`). Populate `\"provider\"`, `\"function_name\"`, and `\"comm_inputs\"`.\n      - IF ANY Gate FAILS -> Classify as a Command Playbook step (`\"step_type\": \"command\"`). Translate GUI/text steps into exact Windows/Linux CLI shell commands with `\"shell\": \"powershell\"`.\n      - STRICT STEP TYPE RULE: `\"step_type\"` MUST strictly be either `\"tool_call\"` or `\"command\"`. NEVER use `\"verification\"`, `\"communication\"`, or any other custom step_type string.\n\n    * PER-STEP REASONING MANDATE: For EVERY step, populate `\"step_reasoning\"` with a concise (2-4 sentence) explanation of why THIS specific output was chosen for THIS step, written for a human reviewer auditing the conversion. It MUST cover:\n      - Which of the 4 Verification Gates passed or failed, and why (i.e. why `tool_call` vs `command` was chosen here).\n      - If `tool_call`: why this exact `provider`/`function_name` was selected over any other catalog entry that looked plausible, and where each `comm_inputs` value came from (raw SOP text, an inferred entity, or a prior step's `output_variable`).\n      - If `command`: why this exact CLI command (and any `{{{{entities.*}}}}`/`{{{{variables.*}}}}` placeholders) was the correct translation of the SOP instruction.\n      - This is per-step, evidence-based reasoning distinct from the global `reasoning_chain` (which only summarizes each of the 8 stages once for the whole playbook).\n\n2. **Stage 2 (Diagnostic & Remediation Sequence Ordering)**: Ensure non-destructive search/diagnostic steps (e.g. `search_users` or `ipconfig`) come FIRST before any corrective or account modification commands.\n\n3. **Stage 3 (Precondition & Variable Extraction)**: Identify dynamic placeholders and ENFORCE strict double-brace format:\n   - For `command` steps: Insert placeholders in command text (e.g. `{{{{entities.hostname}}}}`, `{{{{entities.executable}}}}`).\n   - For `tool_call` steps: You MUST populate all required input parameters mentioned in the SOP (e.g. `current_password`, `email`, `user_id`) inside `comm_inputs` (e.g. `{{{{ \"password\": \"{{{{entities.current_password}}}}\" }}}}`).\n   - Use `{{{{entities.var_name}}}}` for pre-configured entities (`{{{{entities.user_id}}}}`, `{{{{entities.hostname}}}}`, `{{{{entities.email}}}}`, `{{{{entities.current_password}}}}`) and `{{{{variables.var_name}}}}` for runtime step outputs. DO NOT use un-nested single braces.\n\n4. **Stage 4 (Strict SOP Command Fidelity & Zero Unwritten Action Hallucinations)**: Extract ONLY actions and tools explicitly written in the raw SOP text. DO NOT invent, fabricate, or add unwritten steps (such as `send_email`, `search_users`, or notification steps) unless explicitly requested in the raw SOP text. If not explicitly specified in the raw SOP, set `\"fallback_command\": null`.\n\n5. **Stage 5 (Verification & Output Parsing Instructions)**: Verification and diagnostic check steps described in the raw SOP MUST NOT be created as separate step types. Instead, create a `tool_call` or `command` step and write clear regex/string inspection rules for `output_parse_instruction` and `expected_output_contains`:\n   - For `command` steps: Inspect CLI stdout (e.g. \"If output contains 'SUCCESS', set 'kill_confirmed' to 'true'\").\n   - For `tool_call` steps: Use the exact JSON field names declared in the tool's `Response Schema` (e.g. \"If response contains status='unlocked', set 'unlock_success' to 'true' and store response.user_id in variables.user_id\").\n\n6. **Stage 6 (100% Diagnostic Step Recall Mandate)**: You MUST extract EVERY SINGLE step described in the procedure section of the raw SOP text. Omitting any diagnostic or verification step is STRICTLY PROHIBITED.\n\n7. **Stage 7 (Conditional Branch Mapping & execute_if)**: If the raw SOP specifies conditional skip instructions, map this logic into valid `execute_if` boolean expressions (e.g., `execute_if: variables.account_found == 'true'`).\n\n8. **Stage 8 (Dynamic Timeout Allocation by Action Scope)**: Assign `timeout_seconds` dynamically: Quick Info Read / API Call (30s), System Scan / Diagnostic Audit (90s), Full Repair / Heavy Job (300s).\n\n9. **CRITICAL MANDATE (Omit GUI Window Setup Steps)**: DO NOT generate non-executable GUI setup steps like \"Open Command Prompt\" or \"Open Web Browser\". Start directly with functional actions.\n\n10. **CRITICAL MANDATE (Zero Unwritten Action Addition)**: You are STRICTLY FORBIDDEN from inventing or adding steps/tool calls (such as `send_email` or `search_users`) that are NOT explicitly stated in the raw SOP document.\n\n11. **CRITICAL MANDATE (Omit Manual Human/Verbal Confirmation Steps)**: Human/verbal/ticket confirmation checks, manual admin approvals, and user clarification steps are currently PAUSED. Omit non-automated verbal/ticket confirmation steps and start directly with automated API tool calls (`tool_call`) and CLI system commands (`command`).\n\n\n## OUTPUT JSON SCHEMA MANDATE\nCRITICAL FORMATTING MANDATE:\n- All keys and string values MUST be enclosed in standard DOUBLE QUOTES (\").\n- NEVER use Python single quotes (') for dictionary keys or strings.\n- Return raw, valid JSON only matching this schema:\n```json\n{{\n    \"reasoning_chain\": [\n        \"[Stage 1 Verification Gates]: Gate 1 (Function match) -> Gate 2 (Provider active) -> Gate 3 (Problem aligned) -> Gate 4 (Params complete) -> Result: tool_call | command\",\n        \"[Stage 2 Sequencing]: Ordered search/diagnostic steps before remediation\",\n        \"[Stage 3 Variables]: Formatted {{{{entities.var}}}} placeholders\",\n        \"[Stage 4 Fallbacks]: Mapped fallback actions\",\n        \"[Stage 5 Verification]: Added output parse instructions\",\n        \"[Stage 6 Recall]: Preserved 100% of diagnostic steps\",\n        \"[Stage 7 Branching]: Mapped execute_if conditions\",\n        \"[Stage 8 Timeouts]: Assigned timeout_seconds\"\n    ],\n    \"sop_id\": \"SOP-{{file_name_slug}}-001\",\n    \"title\": \"Title of Playbook\",\n    \"category\": \"network | storage | system | logins | software | hardware\",\n    \"target_os\": \"windows | linux\",\n    \"description\": \"Comprehensive overview of what this playbook does\",\n    \"required_entities\": [\"user_id\", \"email\", \"hostname\"],\n    \"trigger_words\": [\"unlock\", \"account\", \"login\"],\n    \"steps\": [\n        {{\n            \"step_number\": 1,\n            \"sop_step_description\": \"Detailed step objective\",\n            \"step_type\": \"tool_call | command\",\n            \"step_reasoning\": \"Gate 1-4 result and why tool_call/command was chosen; why this exact provider/function/command and comm_inputs/placeholders were used over alternatives.\",\n            \"platform\": \"high-level vendor/platform inferred from SOP or tool catalog (only for tool_call, else null)\",\n            \"provider\": \"active_provider_name (from REGISTERED API TOOLS CATALOG above, only for tool_call, else null)\",\n            \"function_name\": \"exact_function_name (from REGISTERED API TOOLS CATALOG above, only for tool_call, else null)\",\n            \"comm_inputs\": {{\n                \"user_id\": \"{{{{entities.user_id}}}}\"\n            }},\n            \"command\": \"ipconfig /all (only for command, else null)\",\n            \"shell\": \"powershell (only for command, else null)\",\n            \"fallback_command\": null,\n            \"fallback_sop_id\": null,\n            \"expected_output_contains\": \"unlocked\",\n            \"timeout_seconds\": 30,\n            \"output_variable\": \"step_result\",\n            \"output_parse_instruction\": \"Inspect output/JSON response and set status flags.\",\n            \"execute_if\": null,\n            \"is_dangerous\": false,\n            \"danger_warning\": null\n        }}\n    ]\n}}\n```\n\n## RAW SOP DOCUMENT TEXT TO TRANSLATE:\nReset Your Password via the Self-Service Portal\nPurpose\nEmployees who forget their password can restore access in minutes through the self-service portal, without opening a helpdesk ticket.\nSkipping the portal and calling the helpdesk directly adds ticket volume and delays access longer than the self-service flow.\nProcedure\nInputs\nCompany email address or employee ID\nAccess to your registered mobile phone or backup email, for the verification code\nA modern web browser (Chrome, Edge, or Safari)\nSteps\nStep 1: Sign-in page opened\nNavigation: Browser address bar → passwordreset.corp.example.com\nEnter your company email address in the box, then select Next. ➡️ Trouble signing in? See the [IT Help Center](https://support.microsoft.com/en-US/accounts-billing/manage/reset-a-forgotten-microsoft-account-password).\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 1 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nVLM Analysis FAILED (non-retryable error): [503] {'message': 'ResourceExhausted: Worker local total request limit reached (6280/16)', 'type': 'Service Unavailable', 'code': 503}\n{'error': {'message': 'ResourceExhausted: Worker local total request limit reached (6280/16)', 'type': 'Service Unavailable', 'code': 503}, 'status': 503}\n\nStep 2: Identity verified\nNavigation: Verify Your Identity screen → Send code button\nPick SMS or email delivery, then enter the 6-digit code you receive. ⚠️ The code expires in 5 minutes — request a new one if it lapses.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 2 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.98 / High]:\n**1. SUMMARY**  \nThe image displays a web‑based “Verify Your Identity” page where a user can request a 6‑digit verification code via SMS (selected) or email and then enter the code to proceed.\n\n**2. DETAILED DESCRIPTION**  \n- **Application/Interface**: A web browser (macOS window with red, yellow, green traffic‑light buttons) showing a corporate password‑reset verification page at `passwordreset.corp.example.com/verify`.  \n- **Current Screen**: A centered white card titled “Verify Your Identity”. It presents a choice of delivery method, an input field for a 6‑digit code, and a verification button.  \n- **Layout & UI Elements**:  \n  - Heading “Verify Your Identity” (large bold text).  \n  - Instruction text: “Choose how’d you’d like to receive your code”.  \n  - Two radio‑button options: “Send code via SMS” (selected, blue outline) and “Send code via Email” (unselected).  \n  - Label “6-digit code” above a text input field that currently shows six masked dots.  \n  - Blue “Verify” button below the input field.  \n  - Small orange/brown text at the bottom: “Code expires in 5 minutes”.  \n- **Visual Conditions**: No pop‑ups, error banners, or loading indicators are visible; the page appears fully loaded and interactive.\n\n**3. EXTRACTED TEXT**  \n```\npasswordreset.corp.example.com/verify\nVerify Your Identity\nChoose how'd you'd like to receive your code\nSend code via SMS\nSend code via Email\n6-digit code\nVerify\nCode expires in 5 minutes\n```\n\n**4. ERRORS AND WARNINGS**  \nnone visible\n\n**5. LIKELY ISSUE CATEGORY AND SEVERITY**  \n- **Category**: Account/authentication  \n- **Severity**: Medium  \n- **Reason**: The user is at the password‑reset verification step; the issue involves receiving or entering a verification code, which is a core authentication process but not a critical system failure.\n\n**6. OBSERVED FACTS VS INFERENCE**  \n\n*OBSERVED FACTS*  \n- The browser window shows macOS traffic‑light controls.  \n- URL bar displays `passwordreset.corp.example.com/verify`.  \n- Page heading reads “Verify Your Identity”.  \n- Two radio‑button options are present; “Send code via SMS” is selected.  \n- A text input field labeled “6-digit code” contains six masked characters (dots).  \n- A blue button labeled “Verify” is present.  \n- Text at the bottom states “Code expires in 5 minutes”.  \n- No error messages, alerts, or loading states are shown.\n\n*INFERENCE*  \n- The user is in the middle of a password‑reset flow and needs to obtain and enter a verification code.  \n- Because “Send code via SMS” is selected, the system is expected to send the code to a mobile phone number on file.  \n- The masked input indicates that no code has been entered yet.  \n- The expiration notice implies the user must complete the verification within 5 minutes of receiving the code.\n\n**7. CONFIDENCE SCORE & NOTES**  \nCONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.98  \nCONFIDENCE LEVEL: High  \nCONFIDENCE REASON: All text is clearly legible, UI elements are fully visible, and the context of a verification page is unambiguous.\n\nStep 3: New password set\nNavigation: Create New Password screen → New Password / Confirm New Password fields\nEnter a new password that meets the company [Password Policy (NIST SP 800-63B)](https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-4/sp800-63b.html), then select Reset Password.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 3 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.96 / High]:\n**1. SUMMARY**  \nThe image shows a web‑based “Create New Password” form on the URL `passwordreset.corp.example.com/reset`. The form contains fields for entering and confirming a new password, a green strength indicator stating “Strong — meets password policy,” and a blue “Reset Password” button.\n\n**2. DETAILED DESCRIPTION**  \n- **Application/Interface:** A browser window (macOS style with red, yellow, green window controls) displaying a secure password‑reset page.  \n- **Current Screen/State:** The “Create New Password” page is active. Both password fields appear filled (masked with dots).  \n- **Layout & UI Elements:**  \n  - A white card centered on a light‑gray background.  \n  - Heading “Create New Password” in bold.  \n  - Two input fields: “New password” and “Confirm new password,” each showing a series of dots indicating hidden characters.  \n  - A horizontal progress bar beneath the fields; the left portion is green, the remainder gray.  \n  - Text below the bar: “Strong — meets password policy” in green.  \n  - A prominent blue button labeled “Reset Password.”  \n- **Visual Conditions:** No visible error messages, pop‑ups, or loading indicators. The interface appears fully rendered and interactive.\n\n**3. EXTRACTED TEXT**  \n```\npasswordreset.corp.example.com/reset\nCreate New Password\nNew password\nConfirm new password\nStrong — meets password policy\nReset Password\n```\n\n**4. ERRORS AND WARNINGS**  \nnone visible\n\n**5. LIKELY ISSUE CATEGORY AND SEVERITY**  \n- **Category:** Account/authentication  \n- **Severity:** Low  \n- **Reason:** The page loads correctly, the password fields are filled, and the strength indicator confirms the password meets policy, suggesting the user is at the final step of a successful reset process.\n\n**6. OBSERVED FACTS VS INFERENCE**  \n\n*OBSERVED FACTS:*  \n- The browser address bar shows `passwordreset.corp.example.com/reset`.  \n- The page title “Create New Password” is displayed.  \n- Two password input fields contain masked characters (dots).  \n- A green progress bar indicates password strength.  \n- Text “Strong — meets password policy” is shown in green.  \n- A blue “Reset Password” button is present and appears clickable.  \n\n*INFERENCE:*  \n- The user has likely entered a password that satisfies the system’s complexity requirements.  \n- The “Reset Password” button is intended to submit the new password and complete the reset process.  \n- No error state is visible, so the issue (if any) is not apparent from this screenshot alone.\n\n**7. CONFIDENCE SCORE & NOTES**  \nCONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.96  \nCONFIDENCE LEVEL: High  \nCONFIDENCE REASON: The visual elements (URL, form fields, button, strength indicator) are clearly legible and fully visible; no ambiguous or missing content affects interpretation.\n\nStep 4: Reset confirmed\nA confirmation email arrives at your registered address. ✅ Sign in with the new password to confirm it worked.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 4 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.98 / High]:\n**1. SUMMARY**  \nThe image displays a web page confirming that a password reset was successful.\n\n**2. DETAILED DESCRIPTION**  \n- **Application/Interface:** A web browser window (macOS Safari) showing a simple HTML page.  \n- **Current Screen/State:** A confirmation screen after a password reset operation.  \n- **Layout & UI Elements:**  \n  - Light gray background with a centered white rectangular card.  \n  - Top‑left of the card: a green circle with a white checkmark icon.  \n  - Bold heading “Password Reset Successful”.  \n  - Two lines of body text below the heading.  \n  - No visible buttons, links, or additional controls on the page.  \n- **Visual Conditions:** Clean, high‑contrast display; no error highlights, pop‑ups, or loading indicators.\n\n**3. EXTRACTED TEXT**  \npasswordreset.corp.example.com/done  \nPassword Reset Successful  \nA confirmation email has been sent to your registered address.  \nSign in with your new password to confirm access.\n\n**4. ERRORS AND WARNINGS**  \nnone visible\n\n**5. LIKELY ISSUE CATEGORY AND SEVERITY**  \n- **Category:** Account/authentication  \n- **Severity:** Low  \n- **Reason:** The page explicitly states the password reset succeeded; there is no error or failure indicated.\n\n**6. OBSERVED FACTS VS INFERENCE**  \n\n*OBSERVED FACTS:*  \n- The browser window shows macOS traffic‑light buttons (red, yellow, green) in the top‑left corner.  \n- The address bar contains “passwordreset.corp.example.com/done”.  \n- A green checkmark icon appears next to the heading “Password Reset Successful”.  \n- Two sentences of text inform the user about an email being sent and the need to sign in with a new password.  \n\n*INFERENCE:*  \n- The user has completed a password‑reset request and received confirmation that the process succeeded.  \n- The system has dispatched a confirmation email to the user’s registered email address.  \n- The user is expected to follow the email instructions to set a new password and regain access.\n\n**7. CONFIDENCE SCORE & NOTES**  \nCONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.98  \nCONFIDENCE LEVEL: High  \nCONFIDENCE REASON: All text is fully legible, UI elements are clear, and the context (successful password reset) is unambiguous.\n\nOutputs\nUser successfully signs in with the new password\nConfirmation email received at the registered address\n✅ No helpdesk ticket required\nFAQs\nQ: What if I don't receive the verification code?\nCheck your spam folder and wait two minutes before requesting a resend. If it still doesn't arrive, contact [IT Support](https://support.microsoft.com/contactus).\nQ: What if I no longer have access to my registered phone or backup email?\nSelf-service reset isn't possible without a verification channel. Open a ticket with IT Support so they can verify your identity manually.\n\n## LINKED REFERENCE KNOWLEDGE (Scraped from embedded SOP URLs):\n### Reference Web Page [https://support.microsoft.com/en-US/accounts-billing/manage/reset-a-forgotten-microsoft-account-password]:\nURL: https://support.microsoft.com/en-US/accounts-billing/manage/reset-a-forgotten-microsoft-account-password\nStatus Code: 200\n\n--- SCRAPED MARKDOWN CONTENT START ---\nSkip to main content\n\n[![](https://uhf.microsoft.com/images/microsoft/RE1Mu3b.png)\nMicrosoft](https://www.microsoft.com)\n\nSupport\n\n[Support](https://support.microsoft.com/en-us)\n\nSupport\n\n* [Home](https://support.microsoft.com/en-us)\n* [Microsoft 365](https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-365?ocid=cmmttvzgpuy)\n* [Office](https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/microsoft-office?ocid=cmml7tr0rib)\n* Products\n  + [Microsoft 365](https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/)\n  + [Outlook](https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/outlook/)\n  + [Microsoft Teams](https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/teams/)\n  + [OneDrive](https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/onedrive/)\n  + [Microsoft Copilot](https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-copilot/)\n  + [OneNote](https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/onenote/)\n  + [Windows](https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/)\n  + [more ...](https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/all-products-list)\n* Devices\n  + [Surface](https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/surface/)\n  + [PC Accessories](https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/surface/accessories/pc/)\n  + [Xbox](https://support.xbox.com/)\n  + [PC Gaming](https://support.xbox.com/help/games-apps/my-games-apps/all-about-pc-gaming)\n  + [HoloLens](https://docs.microsoft.com/hololens/)\n  + [Surface Hub](https://learn.microsoft.com/surface-hub/)\n \n\n### Reference Web Page [https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-4/sp800-63b.html]:\nURL: https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-4/sp800-63b.html\nStatu